.\" Original maux page, MIT, 2026-09-25; rewritten 2026-09-30 for the
.\" tenant launch. No Research UNIX or early BSD counterpart exists:
.\" program selectors (Apple's Bye, Bird's Better Bye) are period
.\" Apple II software, not Unix, and none round-trips a running system.
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH BYE 1 "September 30, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
bye \- run a ProDOS 8 program on the bare machine and come back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bye
[
.I path.system
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Bye
suspends maux, gives the whole machine to a real ProDOS 8, and takes
it back when the program that ran there quits.
Main memory and the auxiliary bank are saved into RamWorks banks
that ProDOS cannot see and restored from them afterwards; every
other process stays in its own bank, untouched.
Nothing else on the machine notices; the shell, the editor and every
other process resume where they were.
.PP
A disk image mounted with
.IR mount (1)
(on /dev/s3d2) is detached before the program runs and /RAM is
handed over as ProDOS made it, and attached again when
.B bye
returns, the same file, the same unit. The program may have changed or
deleted the image: if it no longer attaches,
.B bye
says why ("bye: loop device: no such file") and exits 1, with the
image left unmounted.
.PP
With no operand,
.B bye
makes the round trip and returns at once.
This is a self-test of the mechanism.
.PP
With
.I path.system
the named file is launched as a ProDOS 8 system program, as if a
program selector had chosen it: it must be a type SYS ($FF) file of
no more than 30720 bytes, and it is loaded at $2000 and entered
there.
What it finds is an ordinary freshly booted ProDOS 8: main memory,
the language card and /RAM (reinstalled and empty) all its own,
every open file closed, and the prefix set to the directory the
file was launched from.
When it issues the MLI QUIT call, maux returns.
A program that never quits -- most games, and anything that reboots
the machine to exit -- ends the session; see BUGS.
.PP
Before leaving,
.B bye
closes every file maux has open and remembers their positions; after
the trip they are reopened where they were.
A file that cannot be closed and reopened by name (one opened by a
relative path with no working directory recorded, which the kernel
pins) makes
.B bye
refuse before anything is touched.
.PP
.B Bye
prints
.I Leaving maux for ProDOS...
on the way out and
.I Welcome back.
on the way in.
.SH FILES
.TP
/usr/man/BYE
this page
.TP
/mnt/MAUX/BYERES
the resident that ProDOS's QUIT hands control to
.TP
/mnt/MAUX/KSEG
the kernel's cold segment, reloaded after the trip
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR shutdown (1),
.IR proshim (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 0 when the round trip completes.
.PP
Exit status 2 for a usage error, or for an operand that is not a
type SYS file, is larger than 30720 bytes, or cannot be opened; the
reason is printed and nothing has been touched.
.PP
Exit status 1 when the kernel refuses the trip: a pinned open file
is reported by its file table index, anything else by its errno.
.SH BUGS
The program that runs between the two messages has no exit status
to give back; ProDOS's QUIT carries none.
Whether it succeeded is whatever it left on the volume.
.PP
A program that uses RamWorks memory of its own -- an expanded
AppleWorks, or anything that probes the aux banks -- can overwrite
the banks
.B bye
saved the system into, and there is no way to warn it.
.PP
The saved banks survive a reset, but nothing yet restores from them
after one; a program that never quits costs a reboot.
.PP
A file that grows between the size check and the launch is loaded
truncated at the launch window, not refused.
